---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Section from "../../components/Section.astro";
import SubSection from "../../components/SubSection.astro";
import Image from "../../components/Image.astro";
import Itemize from "../../components/Itemize.astro";
import Item from "../../components/Item.astro";
import InlineEquation from "../../components/InlineEquation.astro";
---

<Layout title="Bonding">
    <div slot="navtree">
        <ul class="list-group list-group-flush py-0">
            <li class="list-group-item py-0">
                <a class="text-decoration-none subsection" href="#ionic-bonding">Ionic Bonding</a>
            </li>
            <li class="list-group-item py-0">
                <a class="text-decoration-none subsection" href="#covalent-bonding">Covalent Bonding</a>
            </li>
            <li class="list-group-item py-0">
                <a class="text-decoration-none subsection" href="#metallic-bonding">Metallic Bonding</a>
            </li>
            <li class="list-group-item py-0">
                <a class="text-decoration-none subsection" href="#secondary-bonds">Secondary Bonds</a>
            </li>
            <li class="list-group-item py-0">
                <a class="text-decoration-none subsection" href="#exercise">Exercise</a>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <Section id="main-section" title="Bonding">
        <p>
            In solids, there are three different types of primary or chemical bonding : <strong>ionic, covalent, and metallic</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Secondary interactions also exist in many materials. Even though these forces are weaker than
            primary bonds, they still influence key properties such as melting behavior, stiffness, and
            thermal response.
        </p>
    </Section>

    <SubSection id="ionic-bonding" title="Ionic Bonding">
        <p>
            Ionic bonding is common in compounds containing metallic and nonmetallic elements.
            A metallic atom tends to donate valence electron(s), while a nonmetal accepts them, producing
            oppositely charged ions that attract each other electrostatically.
        </p>
        <Image src="/advmseintro/Bonding_im1.jpg" width="6"></Image>
    </SubSection>

    <SubSection id="covalent-bonding" title="Covalent Bonding">
        <p>
            Covalent bonding occurs when atoms share valence electrons. This is typical when atoms have
            relatively small electronegativity differences and neither atom fully transfers electrons to the
            other.
        </p>
        <Image src="/advmseintro/Bonding_im2.jpg" width="6"></Image>
    </SubSection>

    <SubSection id="metallic-bonding" title="Metallic Bonding">
        <p>
            In metallic bonding, valence electrons are not tied to one specific atom. Instead, electrons are
            delocalized across many atoms, forming an electron "sea" that contributes to conductivity and
            ductility in metals and alloys.
        </p>
    </SubSection>

    <SubSection id="secondary-bonds" title="Secondary Bonds (Van der Waals)">
        <p>
            Secondary bonds are weaker interactions present in many atomic and molecular systems.
            Although weaker than ionic/covalent/metallic bonds, they can strongly affect material behavior
            when primary bonding is limited or when molecular structures dominate.
        </p>
    </SubSection>

    <SubSection id="exercise" title="Exercise">
        <Image src="/advmseintro/Bonding_im3.jpg" width="6"></Image>
        <p>Using the periodic table, identify the dominant bonding type for each material below.</p>
        <Itemize>
            <Item>Brass (copper-zinc alloy)</Item>
            <Item>Rubber</Item>
            <Item>Calcium fluoride (CaF<InlineEquation equation="_2" />)</Item>
            <Item>Bronze</Item>
            <Item>Solid xenon</Item>
            <Item>Tungsten</Item>
            <Item>Cadmium telluride (CdTe)</Item>
        </Itemize>
    </SubSection>
</Layout>